35,2,1,talk,Midori,,It seems some of our past successes were temporary because the formal agreements took too long to finalize. We need a program that doesn't rely on slow paperwork.,"{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"I found that some previous trials, like the call-back system, actually worked quite well for the patients, but were simply let go. It's a waste of knowledge.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Misaki,,"The staffing situation is worrying. Ward nursing is severely understaffed, though midwifery seems to have some breathing room. We shouldn't add to the ward's burden.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Shion,,"Interesting. The budget is smaller than usual, and we're being charged for floor space. If we build something on-site, we lose money on the fit-out but gain on tariffs.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"Oh dear, I've found that our parking fills up so early in the morning. I worry that any new program might just lead to more traffic jams.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Misaki, if the ward is struggling, maybe we should look for a program that supports the patients outside the ward or uses the midwifery staff.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree. If we can revive a proven method like the call-back trial, we might improve patient outcomes without needing massive new infrastructure.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Misaki,,That would be a relief. Adding more pressure to the ward nurses would be a mistake. A program that utilizes the slack in midwifery could be a smart move.,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Shion,,"If we want to earn back money, we need to keep the activity on-site. Off-site programs bring in zero tariffs. We need a high-volume, on-site solution.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"But @Shion, if we bring more people on-site, where will they park? And the booking system doesn't even record the unscheduled visits. It's all a bit chaotic.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Midori,,"@Shizue, maybe a program that targets a specific, smaller group of patients would avoid the parking issue while still being effective.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"The call-back trial was specifically for the elderly. It didn't require them to travel, which solves the parking and shuttle problem entirely.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Misaki,,That sounds like the most balanced approach. It doesn't strain the ward nurses and doesn't require more parking.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Shion,,"A call-back program is low-cost to start, but it earns nothing in tariffs because it's not an on-site visit. It's a cost center, not a revenue generator.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Shizue,,I think I prefer a cost center over a parking lot full of frustrated people. A gentle approach is always better for the soul.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
